var e={level:`B2`,type:`true-false`,questions:[{id:`b2-tf-001`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'save time and money', 'managers often worry' and 'feeling isolated'.`,passage:`Remote working has changed the way many companies operate. Employees say they save time and money because they do not need to travel. However, managers often worry that staff are less productive at home, and some workers report feeling isolated from their colleagues.`,statements:[{text:`Employees say remote working saves them time.`,answer:!0},{text:`All managers believe staff work better at home.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some workers feel isolated.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-002`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'almost empty twenty years ago' and 'a group of young artists moved in'.`,passage:`The village of Alden was almost empty twenty years ago. Then a group of young artists moved in and opened small galleries. Today the village attracts thousands of visitors every summer, although local residents complain about the noise and the high prices.`,statements:[{text:`Alden is less popular than it was twenty years ago.`,answer:!1},{text:`Artists played a part in the change.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-003`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'some older customers were unhappy', 'busier than ever', 'remains the best-selling item' and 'has not found the right building yet'.`,passage:`When Maria Lopez took over her grandfather's bakery in a small town near the coast, most people expected her to keep things exactly as they were. Instead, she introduced gluten-free bread and started selling coffee in the mornings. At first, some older customers were unhappy and stopped coming. However, within a year the shop had become busier than ever, mainly because young families and tourists began to visit. Maria still uses her grandfather's recipe for the traditional loaf, which remains the best-selling item. She admits that if she had listened to everyone's advice, she would never have made any changes. Next spring she is planning to open a second shop in the nearby city, although she has not found the right building yet.`,statements:[{text:`Maria made changes that some regular customers did not like.`,answer:!0},{text:`The bakery lost money during Maria's first year.`,answer:!1},{text:`The traditional loaf is no longer on sale.`,answer:!1},{text:`Maria has already chosen a building for her second shop.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-004`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'lives under the sea ice', 'depend on the cod for most of their diet', 'three winters' and 'limit fishing'.`,passage:`Scientists working in the Arctic have discovered that a small fish called the polar cod is far more important to the local food chain than previously thought. The fish lives under the sea ice and feeds on tiny animals that grow there. Seals, whales and seabirds, in turn, depend on the cod for most of their diet. As the ice melts earlier each year, the cod are losing their main hiding place and source of food. Researchers warn that if the population falls sharply, many larger animals could struggle to survive. The team spent three winters collecting data, often working in temperatures below minus thirty degrees. They hope their findings will persuade governments to limit fishing in the area until more is known.`,statements:[{text:`The polar cod lives beneath the sea ice.`,answer:!0},{text:`Seals and whales rely heavily on the polar cod for food.`,answer:!0},{text:`The researchers completed their work in a single winter.`,answer:!1},{text:`The scientists want fishing in the area to be increased.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-005`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'the real figure depends on the person', 'someone who exercises a lot... will need more', 'tea and coffee count too' and 'drink when you feel thirsty'.`,passage:`Many people believe that you need eight glasses of water a day to stay healthy, but doctors say the real figure depends on the person. Someone who exercises a lot or lives in a hot country will obviously need more than an office worker in a cool climate. It is also worth remembering that food provides a surprising amount of water. Fruit and vegetables such as cucumbers, oranges and tomatoes are mostly water, and even bread contains some. Tea and coffee count too, despite the popular idea that they dry you out. The best advice, according to most experts, is simply to drink when you feel thirsty and to check the colour of your urine: if it is pale, you are probably drinking enough.`,statements:[{text:`Doctors agree that everyone needs exactly eight glasses of water a day.`,answer:!1},{text:`People who do a lot of sport usually need more water.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to the text, tea and coffee do not count towards your daily water.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts suggest drinking when you are thirsty.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-006`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'first tested last summer', 'most of them reported higher sales', 'residents... complain' and 'free parking spaces'.`,passage:`The city of Valmont has decided to ban cars from its historic centre on Sundays. The idea was first tested last summer, when the main square and the streets around it were closed to traffic for four weekends. Shop owners had been worried that they would lose customers, but most of them reported higher sales than usual, as people stayed longer and walked from shop to shop. Cyclists and families with children were especially enthusiastic. Not everyone is happy, though. Residents who live in the centre complain that they will have to park their cars outside the area and carry their shopping home. The council has promised to provide free parking spaces for them and a small electric bus that will run every fifteen minutes.`,statements:[{text:`The car ban was tested before the final decision was made.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most shop owners sold less during the test period.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some people living in the centre are unhappy with the decision.`,answer:!0},{text:`The council will charge residents for parking outside the centre.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-007`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'he almost refused', his talk was about 'mountain biking', members told him 'to slow down' and 'he wishes he had joined the club years earlier'.`,passage:`Daniel had always been afraid of public speaking, so when his manager asked him to present the team's results at the annual conference, he almost refused. Instead, he decided to join a local speaking club that met every Tuesday evening. For the first few weeks, he only listened to other members, but eventually he gave a short talk about his favourite hobby, mountain biking. The other members gave him helpful feedback, telling him to slow down and look at the audience. By the time the conference came, Daniel had practised his presentation at least ten times. He was still nervous on the day, but the talk went well and several people asked him questions afterwards. He now says he wishes he had joined the club years earlier.`,statements:[{text:`Daniel accepted his manager's request without hesitation.`,answer:!1},{text:`Daniel's first talk at the club was about his work.`,answer:!1},{text:`Club members advised Daniel to speak more slowly.`,answer:!0},{text:`Daniel regrets not joining the club sooner.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-008`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says officials 'tried to reduce the number of stalls', critics say it 'would destroy part of the city's character', and vendors 'must clean the area before they leave'.`,passage:`Street food has become one of the main attractions for visitors to Bangkok, but in recent years the city authorities have tried to reduce the number of stalls on busy pavements. Officials argue that the stalls block the way for pedestrians and create problems with rubbish. Food lovers, on the other hand, say that removing the stalls would destroy part of the city's character. In some areas, a compromise has been reached: vendors are allowed to sell food only at certain times of the day, usually in the evening, and they must clean the area before they leave. Many tourists now plan their trips around these night markets, where they can try dozens of dishes at low prices. For many vendors, these sales are their family's only income.`,statements:[{text:`The authorities want to increase the number of food stalls.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some people think removing the stalls would harm the city's identity.`,answer:!0},{text:`In some areas vendors can only sell food at certain times.`,answer:!0},{text:`Vendors are not responsible for cleaning after they finish.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-009`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the first group worked 'apart from a lunch break', breaks were 'not to check their phones', the second group 'made fewer mistakes' and 'the study was small'.`,passage:`A new study suggests that taking short breaks during the working day can make people more productive. Researchers followed two groups of office workers for six weeks. The first group worked without stopping, apart from a lunch break, while the second group was asked to take a five-minute break every hour. During these breaks, they were encouraged to stand up, stretch or look out of the window, but not to check their phones. At the end of the study, the second group had completed more tasks and made fewer mistakes. They also said they felt less tired at the end of the day. The researchers admit that the study was small, and they are now planning a larger one involving several companies in different countries.`,statements:[{text:`Both groups of workers were allowed a lunch break.`,answer:!0},{text:`The second group was encouraged to use their phones during breaks.`,answer:!1},{text:`Workers who took regular breaks made more mistakes.`,answer:!1},{text:`The researchers believe their study was too small to be final.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-010`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she worked 'part-time at the hospital' while studying, friends 'thought she was taking a big risk', she 'earned much less' at first, and nursing 'taught her how to listen'.`,passage:`Olivia Grant was a nurse for twenty years before she decided to change careers at the age of forty-five. She had always loved gardening, so she enrolled on a two-year course in landscape design while still working part-time at the hospital. Many of her friends thought she was taking a big risk, especially as she had two teenage children. After finishing the course, Olivia started her own business designing small city gardens. The first year was difficult, and she earned much less than she had as a nurse. Now, five years later, she employs three people and has more work than she can manage. She says that her experience in nursing taught her how to listen to people, which is essential when designing a garden for someone.`,statements:[{text:`Olivia stopped working at the hospital completely while she studied.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some of Olivia's friends were worried about her decision.`,answer:!0},{text:`Olivia earned more in her first year of business than she had as a nurse.`,answer:!1},{text:`Olivia thinks her nursing experience helps her in her new job.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-011`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says these women 'never go to matches', alcohol is banned only in 'family sections', a Dutch club offers 'free tickets to girls under twelve', and attendance 'has risen by around fifteen per cent'.`,passage:`Football clubs in several European countries are trying new ways to attract female fans to their stadiums. Research carried out last year showed that many women who enjoy watching football on television never go to matches because they feel the atmosphere is unwelcoming. In response, some clubs have introduced family sections where alcohol is not allowed, and others have improved facilities such as toilets and lighting around the stadium. One club in the Netherlands has started offering free tickets to girls under twelve who play in local teams. Early results are encouraging: the number of women attending matches has risen by around fifteen per cent at the clubs involved. Club officials say they would like to see the same changes across the whole league within five years.`,statements:[{text:`Many women who like football on TV do not go to matches.`,answer:!0},{text:`All clubs have banned alcohol across the whole stadium.`,answer:!1},{text:`A Dutch club gives free tickets to some young players.`,answer:!0},{text:`The number of female fans has fallen at the clubs involved.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-012`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'more than half said they used their phones in bed', the survey 'cannot prove that phones cause poor sleep', schools advise keeping phones 'out of bedrooms', and children 'seem more relaxed'.`,passage:`Smartphones are often blamed for keeping teenagers awake at night, and a recent survey seems to support this idea. Out of two thousand students aged between thirteen and eighteen, more than half said they used their phones in bed, and almost a third admitted waking up during the night to check messages. Those who did so reported feeling tired at school and finding it harder to concentrate. However, the researchers point out that the survey cannot prove that phones cause poor sleep; it is possible that teenagers who already sleep badly simply use their phones more. Some schools have started advising parents to keep phones out of bedrooms after ten o'clock. A few parents say this rule has led to arguments at first, but most report that their children seem more relaxed.`,statements:[{text:`More than half of the students used their phones in bed.`,answer:!0},{text:`The survey proves that phones cause poor sleep.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some schools suggest removing phones from bedrooms at night.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to the text, most parents found the rule made their children more stressed.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-013`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it 'started as a gathering... in a pub', it 'has grown into' a large event, it 'has kept its focus on traditional folk music', and the campsite is 'for the first time' this year.`,passage:`Every August, the small Scottish town of Kirkmuir hosts a music festival that started as a gathering of a few local musicians in a pub. Over thirty years, it has grown into an event that attracts around twenty thousand people. Unlike many large festivals, Kirkmuir has kept its focus on traditional folk music, although organisers have recently added a stage for younger bands who mix folk with rock and electronic sounds. Most of the work is done by volunteers, many of whom live in the town. The festival brings a lot of money into the area, but it also causes problems, such as traffic jams and a shortage of accommodation. This year, for the first time, the organisers have set up a campsite on a farm outside the town.`,statements:[{text:`The festival began in a pub.`,answer:!0},{text:`The festival has always attracted around twenty thousand people.`,answer:!1},{text:`Organisers have stopped including traditional folk music.`,answer:!1},{text:`A campsite has been provided before this year.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-014`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says users 'leave it there' at their destination, injuries were 'mostly among riders who were not wearing helmets', Paris residents 'decided to ban rented scooters', and supporters say 'better rules, not bans'.`,passage:`Electric scooters have appeared on the streets of many cities, and they are popular with commuters who want a quick, cheap way to travel short distances. Users can unlock a scooter with an app, ride it to their destination and leave it there. However, the scooters have also caused a number of problems. Pedestrians complain about scooters being left in the middle of pavements, and hospitals have reported an increase in injuries, mostly among riders who were not wearing helmets. Some cities have responded by limiting the maximum speed to twenty kilometres per hour and creating special parking areas. Paris went further and held a public vote in which residents decided to ban rented scooters altogether. Supporters of the scooters argue that better rules, not bans, are the answer.`,statements:[{text:`Scooter users must return the scooter to the place where they rented it.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most injuries involved riders without helmets.`,answer:!0},{text:`Paris residents voted to ban rented scooters.`,answer:!0},{text:`Supporters of scooters believe bans are the best solution.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-015`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the journey 'took almost seven hours', 'many experts said it could not be done', the line opened 'just in time for the Olympic Games', and lines have been built 'in many countries'.`,passage:`In the 1960s, a group of engineers at a small company in Japan was asked to design a train that could travel between Tokyo and Osaka in under four hours. At the time, the journey took almost seven hours. Many experts said it could not be done, as no train in the world had ever reached the speeds that would be needed. Nevertheless, the engineers succeeded, and the first high-speed line opened in 1964, just in time for the Olympic Games held in Tokyo that year. The new trains were not only fast but also extremely punctual, and they soon became a symbol of modern Japan. Since then, high-speed railways have been built in many countries, including France, Spain and China.`,statements:[{text:`Before the new line, the journey between Tokyo and Osaka took about seven hours.`,answer:!0},{text:`All experts were confident that the project would succeed.`,answer:!1},{text:`The first high-speed line opened before the Tokyo Olympic Games.`,answer:!0},{text:`Japan is the only country with high-speed railways.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-016`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'she felt lonely', 'her landlady suggested' the group, members were 'people of all ages', and 'she still volunteers twice a month'.`,passage:`Lena moved to Berlin from a small village in Austria to study architecture. During her first month, she felt lonely because she did not know anyone and found the city overwhelming. Her landlady suggested she should join a volunteer group that cleaned up parks at weekends. Lena was not very enthusiastic, but she agreed to try it once. To her surprise, she enjoyed it a lot. The group included people of all ages and from many different countries, and after each clean-up they usually had lunch together. Through the group, Lena met her two closest friends and even found a part-time job in a café run by one of the members. She still volunteers twice a month, even though her studies keep her very busy.`,statements:[{text:`Lena felt lonely when she first arrived in Berlin.`,answer:!0},{text:`Lena's landlady suggested the volunteer group.`,answer:!0},{text:`Everyone in the volunteer group was a student.`,answer:!1},{text:`Lena has stopped volunteering because she is too busy.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-017`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says coffee grows 'near the equator', land 'could be reduced by half', farmers move 'higher up the mountains', and some wild species 'do not taste as good'.`,passage:`Coffee is one of the most traded products in the world, but climate change could make it much harder to grow. Most coffee is produced in a narrow band of countries near the equator, where temperatures and rainfall are just right for the plants. As temperatures rise, many of these areas could become too hot or too dry. Some studies suggest that by 2050 the land suitable for growing coffee could be reduced by half. Farmers are already trying to adapt by moving their plantations higher up the mountains, where it is cooler, or by planting trees to provide shade. Scientists are also testing wild coffee species that can survive higher temperatures, although some of them do not taste as good as the varieties people drink today.`,statements:[{text:`Most coffee is grown in countries close to the equator.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies show that coffee production will certainly double by 2050.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some farmers are moving their plantations to higher ground.`,answer:!0},{text:`All the wild coffee species being tested taste better than current varieties.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-018`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'without losing any pay', 'the number of sick days fell', restaurants 'find it more difficult to organise', and 'more than ninety per cent decided to continue'.`,passage:`Many companies now offer their employees the chance to work a four-day week without losing any pay. Supporters say that staff become more focused and efficient when they know they have an extra day off. In a trial involving sixty British companies, most firms reported that productivity stayed the same or even improved, and the number of sick days fell. Workers said they had more time for their families and hobbies. However, the system does not suit every business. Hospitals, shops and restaurants, which need staff every day, find it more difficult to organise. Some managers also worry that customers might be unhappy if they cannot contact the company on Fridays. Of the companies in the trial, more than ninety per cent decided to continue with the four-day week.`,statements:[{text:`Employees in the trial worked fewer days for less money.`,answer:!1},{text:`The number of days workers were ill went down.`,answer:!0},{text:`The text suggests the four-day week is easy for restaurants to organise.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most companies in the trial kept the new system.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-019`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says queues form 'when the weather is good', waiting 'can be very dangerous', waste does 'not decompose at such low temperatures', and climbers 'lose their deposit'.`,passage:`Mount Everest has become so popular with climbers that it now suffers from serious overcrowding. On days when the weather is good, hundreds of people try to reach the summit, and long queues form on the narrow paths near the top. Waiting in such extreme cold and with so little oxygen can be very dangerous. In 2019, several climbers died after spending hours in a queue. Another problem is waste: climbers leave behind tents, oxygen bottles and other rubbish, which do not decompose at such low temperatures. The government of Nepal now requires every climber to bring down at least eight kilograms of rubbish, or they lose their deposit. Some people have suggested limiting the number of permits, but this would reduce an important source of income for the country.`,statements:[{text:`Queues near the summit only form when the weather is bad.`,answer:!1},{text:`Waiting near the top of Everest can be life-threatening.`,answer:!0},{text:`Rubbish on Everest breaks down quickly because of the cold.`,answer:!1},{text:`Climbers who do not bring down rubbish can lose money.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-020`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he opened it 'after losing his job', he sells 'at fair prices', the workshops are 'free', and he 'has had to introduce a booking system'.`,passage:`Ahmed opened a small bicycle repair shop in his neighbourhood five years ago, after losing his job at a car factory. At first he only fixed bikes, but he soon noticed that many people had old bicycles at home that they never used. He started buying these bicycles cheaply, repairing them and selling them at fair prices to students and people on low incomes. He also runs free workshops on Saturday mornings, where he teaches people how to fix punctures and adjust their brakes. The workshops have become so popular that he has had to introduce a booking system. Last year the local council gave Ahmed an award for his contribution to the community, and a national newspaper wrote an article about him.`,statements:[{text:`Ahmed opened his shop after he lost his previous job.`,answer:!0},{text:`Ahmed sells repaired bicycles at very high prices.`,answer:!1},{text:`People have to pay to attend Ahmed's Saturday workshops.`,answer:!1},{text:`Ahmed's workshops are now so popular that people need to book.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-021`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says headsets show 'what an ancient city looked like', the game is for 'children', visits from families 'have increased', and museums keep 'technology optional'.`,passage:`Museums around the world are using new technology to attract younger visitors. Some offer virtual reality headsets that allow people to see what an ancient city looked like thousands of years ago, while others have created apps that tell stories about the objects on display. The Natural History Museum in one European capital recently introduced a game in which children search the building for hidden clues to solve a mystery. Staff say that visits from families have increased considerably since the game was launched. However, not everyone welcomes these changes. Some critics argue that screens distract visitors from the real objects and that museums should encourage people to look closely rather than through a device. Most museums try to find a balance, keeping technology optional.`,statements:[{text:`Some museums let visitors see ancient cities using virtual reality.`,answer:!0},{text:`The game introduced by the Natural History Museum is designed for adults.`,answer:!1},{text:`Family visits went down after the game was launched.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most museums make technology optional for visitors.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-022`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says fermentation is 'one of the oldest ways', 'more research is needed', heating 'kills them', and the process 'can take several weeks'.`,passage:`Fermented foods such as yoghurt, kimchi and sauerkraut have become fashionable in recent years, partly because of claims that they are good for the stomach. Fermentation is one of the oldest ways of preserving food: bacteria or yeast change the sugars in the food, which helps it last longer and gives it a sour taste. Some scientists believe the bacteria in these foods can improve digestion and even strengthen the immune system, but they stress that more research is needed. Not all products sold in supermarkets contain live bacteria, since some are heated during production, which kills them. Experts therefore recommend checking the label carefully. Making fermented vegetables at home is cheap and fairly easy, although it requires patience, as the process can take several weeks.`,statements:[{text:`Fermentation is a modern method of preserving food.`,answer:!1},{text:`Scientists say there is already enough evidence about the health benefits.`,answer:!1},{text:`Heating some products during production kills the bacteria.`,answer:!0},{text:`Making fermented vegetables at home can take weeks.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-023`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the building 'stood empty for almost twenty years', plans to knock it down 'were rejected', the brick walls and columns 'were kept', and 'a quarter of the flats' have lower rents.`,passage:`When the old textile factory in the city of Lowfield closed in the 1990s, the building stood empty for almost twenty years. Several plans to knock it down were rejected because local people wanted to keep it as part of the town's history. Finally, a group of investors bought it and turned it into a centre with flats, offices, a cinema and a covered market. The original brick walls and iron columns were kept, and a small museum on the ground floor tells the story of the factory and its workers. The project was criticised at first because the flats were too expensive for most local families. Following these complaints, the developers agreed that a quarter of the flats would be offered at lower rents.`,statements:[{text:`The factory building was used again soon after it closed.`,answer:!1},{text:`Local people opposed plans to demolish the building.`,answer:!0},{text:`None of the original features of the building were kept.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some of the flats are now available at reduced rents.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-024`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it is 'far from impossible', adults 'may feel embarrassed about making mistakes', teachers prefer 'short periods every day', and playing can 'reduce stress'.`,passage:`Learning a musical instrument as an adult is harder than learning as a child, but research shows it is far from impossible. Adults usually have less free time and may feel embarrassed about making mistakes, which can make them give up quickly. On the other hand, they often have better concentration and a clearer idea of why they want to learn. Teachers recommend practising for short periods every day rather than for several hours once a week. They also suggest choosing music you genuinely enjoy, since motivation is the key to making progress. Studies have found that playing an instrument can improve memory and reduce stress at any age. Many adult learners say that joining a group, such as a community orchestra, helped them keep going.`,statements:[{text:`Research suggests adults cannot learn a musical instrument.`,answer:!1},{text:`Adults may give up because they are embarrassed about their mistakes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Teachers recommend one long practice session per week.`,answer:!1},{text:`Playing an instrument can reduce stress.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-025`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says residents were 'most of them elderly', 'the internet connection was extremely slow', the school 'now has fifteen pupils', and newcomers 'helped to reopen the island's only shop'.`,passage:`The island of Hallsey, off the west coast of Ireland, had only about fifty permanent residents ten years ago, most of them elderly. Young people kept leaving because there were no jobs and the internet connection was extremely slow. Then the island received a grant to install high-speed broadband. Since then, around twenty families have moved there, many of them people who work online for companies based in cities. The primary school, which was about to close, now has fifteen pupils. Not all residents are pleased: some worry that house prices are rising and that the island's quiet way of life could change. The newcomers, however, have helped to reopen the island's only shop and organise events such as a summer food fair.`,statements:[{text:`Ten years ago most of the island's residents were young.`,answer:!1},{text:`Poor internet was one reason young people left the island.`,answer:!0},{text:`The primary school closed after the broadband was installed.`,answer:!1},{text:`New residents helped to reopen the shop.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-026`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says intense exercise 'can make it harder to fall asleep', keep the same times 'even at weekends', sleeping late on Sunday makes Monday 'more difficult', and see a doctor if problems 'continue for more than a few weeks'.`,passage:`Sleep experts say that the hour before bed is just as important as the sleep itself. Bright screens, heavy meals and intense exercise can all make it harder to fall asleep. Instead, they recommend activities that help the body relax, such as reading a paper book, having a warm shower or listening to calm music. Keeping a regular schedule is also important: going to bed and getting up at the same time every day, even at weekends, helps the body's internal clock work properly. Many people try to catch up on sleep at the weekend, but experts warn that sleeping very late on Sunday can make Monday morning even more difficult. If sleep problems continue for more than a few weeks, it is a good idea to see a doctor.`,statements:[{text:`Intense exercise just before bed can make it harder to sleep.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts advise changing your sleep schedule at weekends.`,answer:!1},{text:`Sleeping very late on Sunday can make Monday morning harder.`,answer:!0},{text:`People with sleep problems should see a doctor immediately after one bad night.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-027`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says robots deliver items 'to human workers, who then pack them', orders are processed 'three times faster', unions say 'many workers lost their jobs', and humans 'will focus on work that needs creativity'.`,passage:`Robots are increasingly being used in warehouses to pick and pack goods for online orders. In one large warehouse in the north of England, robots move along a grid on top of storage boxes, lifting the items needed and delivering them to human workers, who then pack them. The company says this system allows it to process orders three times faster than before. Although some jobs have disappeared, the company claims that it has retrained many employees to maintain and repair the robots. Trade unions, however, say that many workers lost their jobs and that the new roles require skills not everyone can easily learn. Experts believe that in the future, robots will take over more routine tasks, while humans will focus on work that needs creativity and judgement.`,statements:[{text:`In the warehouse described, robots do all the packing without human help.`,answer:!1},{text:`The company says orders are processed much faster with robots.`,answer:!0},{text:`Trade unions agree that no workers lost their jobs.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts think humans will focus on creative work in the future.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-028`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says bees 'pollinate many of the crops we eat', 'there is no single cause', people 'keep beehives on rooftops', and wild bees are 'just as important'.`,passage:`Honeybees play a vital role in agriculture because they pollinate many of the crops we eat, including apples, almonds and strawberries. In recent decades, however, beekeepers in Europe and North America have reported losing large numbers of colonies every winter. Scientists believe there is no single cause. Pesticides, diseases, a lack of wild flowers and changes in the weather all seem to play a part. To help, some cities have started planting flowers along roads and in parks, and many people now keep beehives on rooftops. Experts point out, though, that wild bees, which are often forgotten, are just as important as honeybees and may be even more at risk. They encourage gardeners to leave some areas wild and avoid using chemicals.`,statements:[{text:`Honeybees help many food crops to grow.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists have identified one main cause of colony losses.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some people keep beehives on the roofs of buildings.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts say wild bees are less important than honeybees.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-029`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she bought it 'on a sudden impulse', learned skills 'from her neighbours', 'the roof leaked' in the first winter, and she has 'no plans to return to London'.`,passage:`Ten years ago, Sophie Chen was working long hours as an accountant in London when she went on holiday to Portugal and fell in love with a small village in the hills. On a sudden impulse, she bought an old farmhouse that needed a lot of repairs. She spent the next three years restoring it, mostly by herself, learning skills such as plastering and tiling from online videos and from her neighbours. The farmhouse is now a guesthouse with six rooms, and it is fully booked for most of the summer. Sophie admits that there were moments when she regretted the decision, especially during the first winter, when the roof leaked. But she says she would do it all again, and she has no plans to return to London.`,statements:[{text:`Sophie bought the farmhouse after planning carefully for many years.`,answer:!1},{text:`Sophie learned some building skills from her neighbours.`,answer:!0},{text:`The roof of the farmhouse leaked during the first winter.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sophie is planning to move back to London.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-030`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the food is 'still safe to eat', it is sold 'at a discount', users collect bags 'often without knowing exactly what will be inside', and critics say 'the real problem is overproduction'.`,passage:`Many supermarkets throw away large amounts of food that is still safe to eat, simply because it has passed its 'best before' date or does not look perfect. In response, a number of apps have been developed that connect shops and restaurants with customers who want to buy surplus food at a discount. At the end of the day, a bakery might offer a bag of unsold bread and pastries for a third of the normal price. Users collect their bag at a set time, often without knowing exactly what will be inside. The companies behind these apps say they have saved millions of meals from being wasted. Critics, however, argue that the real problem is overproduction and that shops should simply produce less.`,statements:[{text:`Some food that supermarkets throw away is still safe to eat.`,answer:!0},{text:`The apps allow customers to buy surplus food at reduced prices.`,answer:!0},{text:`Users always know exactly what they will receive.`,answer:!1},{text:`Critics believe overproduction is the real problem.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-031`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says races 'receive far more applications than they have places', most runners 'are not trying to win', increasing distance too quickly 'often leads to injury', and experts 'stress the importance of rest days'.`,passage:`Marathon running has become hugely popular among amateurs, and big-city races now receive far more applications than they have places. In some cases, runners have to enter a ballot, and only one in ten applicants is selected. Most participants are not trying to win; they want to raise money for charity or simply prove to themselves that they can finish. Doctors say that training for a marathon can bring great health benefits, but they warn beginners not to increase their distance too quickly, as this often leads to injury. A typical training plan lasts around four months and includes one long run each week. Experts also stress the importance of rest days, which allow the muscles to recover and become stronger.`,statements:[{text:`Big-city marathons have more places than applicants.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most amateur runners are trying to win the race.`,answer:!1},{text:`Increasing distance too fast can cause injuries.`,answer:!0},{text:`Rest days are considered unimportant by experts.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-032`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people used to stay 'with one company for most of their working lives', young workers value flexibility 'more than a high salary', some allow staff to 'choose their own hours', and 'some employers are suspicious'.`,passage:`In the past, most people stayed with one company for most of their working lives. Today, however, it is common for workers to change jobs every few years. Surveys suggest that younger employees in particular value flexibility, learning opportunities and a good work-life balance more than a high salary. Companies have noticed this and are trying harder to keep their staff. Some offer training programmes, while others allow employees to work from home or choose their own hours. Changing jobs frequently can help people gain a wide range of experience and often leads to higher pay. On the other hand, some employers are suspicious of candidates whose CVs show many short jobs, as they fear these people will not stay long.`,statements:[{text:`In the past, people usually changed jobs every few years.`,answer:!1},{text:`Younger workers often care more about flexibility than salary.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some companies let employees choose their own working hours.`,answer:!0},{text:`All employers see frequent job changes as a positive sign.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-033`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says tomatoes came 'from Central and South America', 'many Europeans refused to eat it', the fear in Britain 'lasted longer', and botanists 'classify it as a fruit'.`,passage:`The tomato was brought to Europe from Central and South America in the sixteenth century. At first, many Europeans refused to eat it. Because the plant belongs to the same family as some poisonous plants, people grew it only for decoration. In Italy and Spain, however, cooks gradually began to use it in sauces, and by the eighteenth century it had become a common ingredient in southern European cooking. In Britain and North America, the fear lasted longer, and it was not until the nineteenth century that tomatoes were widely eaten there. Today, the tomato is one of the most widely grown vegetables in the world, although botanists actually classify it as a fruit because it contains seeds.`,statements:[{text:`Tomatoes originally came from the Americas.`,answer:!0},{text:`Europeans began eating tomatoes as soon as they arrived.`,answer:!1},{text:`Tomatoes were accepted in southern Europe before they were in Britain.`,answer:!0},{text:`Botanists consider the tomato to be a vegetable.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-034`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says users can 'compete with friends', research 'is mixed' and one group 'lost slightly less weight', doctors worry it 'can make people anxious', and experts say trackers 'can be useful tools'.`,passage:`Wearable fitness trackers, which count steps, measure heart rate and monitor sleep, are now worn by millions of people. Many users say the devices motivate them to move more, especially when they can compete with friends. However, research on their long-term effects is mixed. One study found that people who wore a tracker for two years lost slightly less weight than those who did not, possibly because they felt they had earned the right to eat more after reaching their step target. Some doctors also worry that constant monitoring can make people anxious about their health. Most experts agree that trackers can be useful tools, but only if people use the information sensibly rather than becoming obsessed with numbers.`,statements:[{text:`Some users are motivated by competing with friends.`,answer:!0},{text:`Research clearly shows that trackers help people lose more weight.`,answer:!1},{text:`Doctors are concerned that monitoring may cause anxiety.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts believe trackers are useless.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-035`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people cycle 'whatever the weather', cycling 'was becoming less common', lanes are 'separated from traffic', and other cities 'have sent planners to Copenhagen'.`,passage:`Copenhagen is often described as one of the most bicycle-friendly cities in the world. More than forty per cent of residents cycle to work or school every day, whatever the weather. This was not always the case. In the 1960s and 1970s, the city was full of cars, and cycling was becoming less common. After the oil crisis of the 1970s, however, residents began demanding safer routes for bikes. The city responded by building wide cycle lanes separated from traffic, special bridges for cyclists and traffic lights timed to suit bikes. Today, local politicians say that cycling saves the city millions in health care costs each year. Other cities, including some in North America, have sent planners to Copenhagen to learn from its experience.`,statements:[{text:`Copenhagen residents only cycle when the weather is good.`,answer:!1},{text:`Cycling became less common in Copenhagen in the 1960s and 1970s.`,answer:!0},{text:`The city built cycle lanes that are separated from cars.`,answer:!0},{text:`Planners from other countries have visited Copenhagen to learn from it.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-036`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he had painted 'at school', his teacher 'encouraged him to enter', his painting 'won second prize', and he gave 'some of the money to a charity'.`,passage:`When Thomas retired from his job as a postman, he was worried about being bored. His daughter suggested he should take up painting, something he had enjoyed at school but had not done for over forty years. He joined an evening class at the local college and was surprised to find that he had not lost his skill. After a year, his teacher encouraged him to enter a regional competition for amateur artists. Thomas did not expect to win anything, but his painting of the harbour at sunrise won second prize. Since then, he has sold several paintings at a local gallery and given some of the money to a charity for older people. He now says that retirement has been the busiest time of his life.`,statements:[{text:`Thomas had never painted before he retired.`,answer:!1},{text:`His teacher suggested he should enter a competition.`,answer:!0},{text:`Thomas won first prize in the competition.`,answer:!1},{text:`Thomas has given some of his earnings to charity.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-037`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the soil 'is extremely fertile', Iceland uses underground heat 'to produce electricity', scientists 'cannot predict eruptions exactly', and their warnings 'have saved many lives'.`,passage:`Volcanoes are often seen only as dangerous, but they also bring benefits. The soil around many volcanoes is extremely fertile, which is why farmers have lived on their slopes for thousands of years, despite the risks. In countries such as Iceland, the heat from underground is used to produce electricity and to warm homes. Volcanic areas also attract large numbers of tourists, who come to see craters, hot springs and lava fields. Scientists monitor active volcanoes closely, measuring small earthquakes and changes in the shape of the ground, which can show that an eruption may be coming. Although they cannot predict eruptions exactly, their warnings have saved many lives by allowing authorities to evacuate people in time.`,statements:[{text:`The soil near volcanoes is usually poor for farming.`,answer:!1},{text:`Iceland uses underground heat to produce electricity.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists can predict the exact time of an eruption.`,answer:!1},{text:`Scientists' warnings have helped to save lives.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-038`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says pupils grow vegetables 'in the school garden', fussy eaters 'are now willing to try new foods', some children 'ask to help cook at home', and 'the main obstacle... is cost'.`,passage:`A growing number of schools are teaching children to cook as part of their regular timetable. In one primary school in Manchester, pupils spend an hour every week in a kitchen classroom, where they learn to prepare simple meals using fresh vegetables, many of which they grow themselves in the school garden. Teachers say that children who were once fussy eaters are now willing to try new foods because they helped to make them. Parents have also noticed changes, with some reporting that their children now ask to help cook at home. The head teacher believes that cooking also teaches maths, science and teamwork. The main obstacle for other schools is cost, as kitchen equipment is expensive and lessons require extra staff.`,statements:[{text:`Pupils grow some of the vegetables they use.`,answer:!0},{text:`Teachers say fussy eaters still refuse to try new foods.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some children now want to help with cooking at home.`,answer:!0},{text:`Cooking lessons are cheap for schools to organise.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-039`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people can study 'for free', apps 'are good for learning vocabulary', they 'rarely give learners enough chance to speak', and combining them with other activities gives 'much faster progress'.`,passage:`Language apps have made it possible for millions of people to study a foreign language for free on their phones. The apps use games, points and daily reminders to encourage users to practise for a few minutes every day. Many users say this approach helps them build a habit. However, language teachers point out that apps are good for learning vocabulary and basic grammar, but they rarely give learners enough chance to speak or to understand natural conversation. Research suggests that people who combine an app with other activities, such as watching films, reading or talking to native speakers, make much faster progress. The most successful learners, according to one study, were those who used the language in real situations as early as possible.`,statements:[{text:`Most language apps charge users a high monthly fee.`,answer:!1},{text:`Teachers say apps are good for learning vocabulary.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to teachers, apps give plenty of speaking practice.`,answer:!1},{text:`Combining apps with other activities leads to faster progress.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-040`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Petra 'was carved directly into pink sandstone cliffs', the Nabataeans 'became rich by controlling trade routes', the traveller 'had to disguise himself', and authorities 'are considering limiting the number of daily tickets'.`,passage:`The ancient city of Petra, in Jordan, was carved directly into pink sandstone cliffs more than two thousand years ago. It was built by the Nabataeans, a people who became rich by controlling trade routes for spices and incense. For centuries after the city was abandoned, it was known only to local tribes. It was rediscovered by a European traveller in 1812, who had to disguise himself to enter. Today, Petra is Jordan's most popular tourist attraction and was named one of the new Seven Wonders of the World in 2007. However, the large number of visitors is causing damage to the soft stone, and the authorities are considering limiting the number of daily tickets to protect the site.`,statements:[{text:`Petra was carved into the rock.`,answer:!0},{text:`The Nabataeans became wealthy through trade.`,answer:!0},{text:`The European traveller entered Petra in 1812 without any disguise.`,answer:!1},{text:`The authorities are thinking about limiting visitor numbers.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-041`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says laughing 'reduces stress hormones', children 'feel less anxious', members 'start by pretending to laugh' for 'no particular reason', and 'it is hard to measure' the effect.`,passage:`Laughter, it seems, really can be good medicine. Studies have found that laughing reduces stress hormones, relaxes the muscles and may even help the heart. In some hospitals, clowns visit children's wards to cheer up young patients, and doctors report that the children often feel less anxious before operations. There are also laughter yoga clubs, where people meet to laugh together for no particular reason. Members start by pretending to laugh, but the fake laughter usually turns into real laughter quite quickly, as it is very contagious. Researchers admit that it is hard to measure exactly how much laughter improves health, since people who laugh a lot may also have other healthy habits, like exercising more or having strong friendships.`,statements:[{text:`Laughing can help to reduce stress.`,answer:!0},{text:`Hospital clowns make children more anxious before operations.`,answer:!1},{text:`In laughter yoga, people begin by laughing at jokes.`,answer:!1},{text:`It is easy to measure exactly how laughter affects health.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-042`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says there are 'not enough' maths and science teachers, some governments are 'paying off student loans', people train 'while working in a school', and the main reasons are 'heavy workloads and a lack of respect, rather than pay'.`,passage:`In many countries, there are not enough people training to become teachers, especially in subjects such as maths and science. Some governments have tried to solve this by offering higher starting salaries or paying off student loans for people who agree to teach for a certain number of years. Others have created programmes that allow people from other careers, such as engineers or scientists, to train as teachers while working in a school. These programmes have brought many talented people into the profession, but critics say that some new teachers are not given enough support and leave after only a year or two. Surveys show that the main reasons teachers give for leaving are heavy workloads and a lack of respect, rather than pay.`,statements:[{text:`There is a shortage of maths and science teachers in many countries.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some governments pay off student loans for new teachers.`,answer:!0},{text:`Career-change programmes require people to stop working while they train.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most teachers who leave say pay is their main reason.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-043`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they travelled 'by bus', it was 'impossible to follow it exactly', 'a local family invited them to a wedding', and they 'visited fewer places than they had intended'.`,passage:`Lucy and her brother Mark spent last summer travelling around South America by bus. They had planned their route carefully, but they soon realised that it was impossible to follow it exactly. Buses were often delayed, and in Bolivia a road was blocked for two days because of a landslide. Instead of getting annoyed, they decided to stay in the small town where they were stuck, and it turned out to be one of the highlights of the trip. A local family invited them to a wedding, where they danced until three in the morning. By the end of the trip, they had visited fewer places than they had intended, but they both agreed it had been the best summer of their lives.`,statements:[{text:`Lucy and Mark travelled around South America by train.`,answer:!1},{text:`They were able to follow their planned route exactly.`,answer:!1},{text:`They were invited to a wedding by a local family.`,answer:!0},{text:`They visited more places than they had planned.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-044`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'rules differ from one town to another', whole loads 'can be contaminated', reducing waste 'is more effective than recycling', and charging 'has led to a significant fall in waste'.`,passage:`Most people know that recycling helps the environment, but many are confused about what can actually be recycled. Rules differ from one town to another, and a plastic tray that can be recycled in one city may have to go in the general rubbish in the next. When people put the wrong items in recycling bins, whole loads can be contaminated and end up being burned or buried. Experts say that reducing waste in the first place is more effective than recycling. This means buying fewer products with unnecessary packaging, repairing things instead of replacing them and using refillable bottles and bags. Some cities have started charging households for the amount of general rubbish they produce, which has led to a significant fall in waste.`,statements:[{text:`Recycling rules are the same in every town.`,answer:!1},{text:`Putting the wrong items in recycling bins can spoil whole loads.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts think recycling is more effective than reducing waste.`,answer:!1},{text:`Charging households for rubbish has helped to reduce waste in some cities.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-045`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Webb took 'almost twenty-two hours', 'only a small proportion have succeeded', currents mean swimmers 'cover much more', and wetsuits are not allowed for official crossings.`,passage:`The first person to swim across the English Channel was Matthew Webb, a British sailor, who completed the crossing in 1875. It took him almost twenty-two hours, and he covered his body in oil to protect himself from the cold water. Since then, thousands of people have attempted the swim, but only a small proportion have succeeded. The straight-line distance is about thirty-four kilometres, but strong currents mean that swimmers usually cover much more. Swimmers are not allowed to wear wetsuits if they want their crossing to be officially recognised; they can only wear a normal swimming costume, a cap and goggles. Every swimmer must also be accompanied by a boat, which guides them and checks their safety.`,statements:[{text:`Matthew Webb took less than twelve hours to cross the Channel.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most people who try to swim the Channel succeed.`,answer:!1},{text:`Swimmers usually swim further than the straight-line distance.`,answer:!0},{text:`Wetsuits are not allowed for an officially recognised crossing.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-046`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says some customers 'order several sizes', items 'are sometimes simply thrown away', some retailers 'have recently started charging a small fee', and virtual fitting rooms help people 'choose the right size'.`,passage:`Online shopping has transformed the way people buy goods, but it has also created a huge increase in returns. Because many shops offer free returns, some customers order several sizes of the same item and send back the ones that do not fit. This is convenient for shoppers, but it is expensive for companies and bad for the environment, as returned items have to be transported again and are sometimes simply thrown away. A few large retailers have recently started charging a small fee for returns, hoping that customers will think more carefully before they buy. Others are using technology, such as virtual fitting rooms, to help people choose the right size the first time.`,statements:[{text:`Some customers order several sizes of the same item.`,answer:!0},{text:`Returned items are never thrown away.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some retailers have started charging customers for returns.`,answer:!0},{text:`Virtual fitting rooms are designed to help customers choose the correct size.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-047`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she made them 'as a hobby', she got 'over five hundred orders in a single week' after the video, managing money and staff 'has been the hardest part', and she 'has turned down offers'.`,passage:`Grace was only nineteen when she started her own business making candles in her parents' garage. She had been making them as a hobby for a couple of years, and her friends kept asking her to sell them. At first, she sold them at local markets, but after a video she posted online went viral, she received over five hundred orders in a single week. She had to ask her whole family to help her pack the parcels. Two years later, the business has moved into a small workshop and employs four people. Grace says the hardest part has been learning to manage money and staff, which she had never done before. She has turned down offers from larger companies who wanted to buy her business.`,statements:[{text:`Grace started making candles as a hobby.`,answer:!0},{text:`Her online video led to a large number of orders.`,answer:!0},{text:`Grace found managing money and staff easy.`,answer:!1},{text:`Grace sold her business to a larger company.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-048`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says over eighty per cent 'can no longer see the Milky Way', lights 'confuse birds', light 'can disturb people's sleep', and it is 'one of the easiest forms of pollution to reduce'.`,passage:`Light pollution, the excess artificial light produced by cities, is becoming a serious problem. It is estimated that more than eighty per cent of the world's population lives under skies so bright that they can no longer see the Milky Way. But the effects go beyond spoiling the view of the stars. Bright lights at night confuse birds that migrate in the dark, and they attract insects, many of which die as a result. Some studies also suggest that exposure to light at night can disturb people's sleep. Fortunately, light pollution is one of the easiest forms of pollution to reduce. Using lights that point downwards, switching them off when they are not needed and choosing warmer colours can make a big difference.`,statements:[{text:`Most people in the world can still see the Milky Way from where they live.`,answer:!1},{text:`Artificial light at night can confuse migrating birds.`,answer:!0},{text:`Light at night may affect people's sleep.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to the text, light pollution is very difficult to reduce.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-049`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says chess 'was dominated by players from the Soviet Union', top players now 'come from all over the world', players reach a high standard 'at a much younger age', and opinions about creativity differ.`,passage:`For most of the twentieth century, chess was dominated by players from the Soviet Union. Today, however, top players come from all over the world, including India, Norway, China and the United States. One reason for this change is the internet. Young players can now play thousands of games online against opponents of every level, and they can study using powerful computer programs that are stronger than any human. As a result, players are reaching a very high standard at a much younger age than before. Some critics feel that computers have made the game less creative, as players often memorise long sequences of moves. Others argue that the computer has actually revealed new and surprising ideas.`,statements:[{text:`Soviet players dominated chess for much of the twentieth century.`,answer:!0},{text:`Today top chess players come only from a few countries.`,answer:!1},{text:`Players are now reaching a high level at a younger age.`,answer:!0},{text:`Everyone agrees that computers have made chess less creative.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-050`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they are 'cheaper to build and heat', some owners can 'move their home', finding legal land 'is also a challenge', and several owners 'moved back to a normal house'.`,passage:`Many people dream of living in a tiny house: a small, often movable home with just enough space for a bed, a kitchen and a bathroom. Supporters say tiny houses are cheaper to build and heat, and that they encourage people to own fewer possessions. Some owners also enjoy the freedom of being able to move their home to a new location. However, living in such a small space is not always easy, especially for families or couples who work from home. In many countries, finding legal land to park a tiny house is also a challenge, as planning laws were not designed for this type of home. Several owners interviewed for a recent report said they had moved back to a normal house after a few years.`,statements:[{text:`Tiny houses are usually more expensive to heat than normal houses.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some tiny houses can be moved to different places.`,answer:!0},{text:`Finding legal land for a tiny house can be difficult.`,answer:!0},{text:`All the owners interviewed still live in tiny houses.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-051`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the lights are 'caused by particles from the sun', the best time is 'between September and March', seeing them 'is never guaranteed', and companies offer 'a second trip for free'.`,passage:`Every year, millions of people travel to see the northern lights, the colourful lights that appear in the sky near the Arctic. They are caused by particles from the sun colliding with gases in the Earth's atmosphere. The best time to see them is during the long, dark nights between September and March, in places such as northern Norway, Finland, Iceland and Canada. However, seeing them is never guaranteed. A clear sky is essential, and the strength of the lights depends on activity on the sun, which changes from day to day. Tour companies often offer to take visitors to a second trip for free if they do not see the lights the first time. Photographers recommend using a tripod and staying out for several hours.`,statements:[{text:`The northern lights are caused by particles from the sun.`,answer:!0},{text:`The best time to see them is during the summer months.`,answer:!1},{text:`Visitors are always certain to see the lights.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some tour companies offer a free second trip.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-052`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he accepted 'without thinking twice', most of his day was 'spent in meetings', his boss 'suggested a compromise' of three days and two days, and the company 'has since introduced the same option'.`,passage:`Jake had worked as a software developer for eight years when he was offered a promotion to team manager. The salary was much higher, so he accepted without thinking twice. Within a few months, however, he realised he missed writing code. Most of his day was now spent in meetings, writing reports and dealing with problems between team members. He spoke to his boss, who suggested a compromise: Jake would spend three days a week managing the team and two days working on technical projects. The arrangement has worked well, and Jake says he feels much happier. His company has since introduced the same option for other managers, recognising that not everyone who is good at their job wants to stop doing it.`,statements:[{text:`Jake took a long time to decide whether to accept the promotion.`,answer:!1},{text:`As a manager, Jake spent most of his time in meetings.`,answer:!0},{text:`Jake's boss suggested dividing his week between two roles.`,answer:!0},{text:`The company now offers the same arrangement to other managers.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-053`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the reef 'can even be seen from space', corals 'can recover' if temperatures return to normal, bleaching happens when corals are 'stressed by heat', and reducing emissions 'is the most important step'.`,passage:`The Great Barrier Reef, off the coast of Australia, is the largest coral reef system in the world and can even be seen from space. It is home to thousands of species of fish, as well as turtles, sharks and dolphins. In recent years, however, rising sea temperatures have caused several mass bleaching events. Bleaching happens when corals become stressed by heat and lose the tiny algae that give them their colour and much of their food. If temperatures return to normal quickly, the corals can recover, but if the heat continues for too long, they die. Scientists are experimenting with ways to help, such as breeding corals that can tolerate warmer water, but they agree that reducing global emissions is the most important step.`,statements:[{text:`The Great Barrier Reef is visible from space.`,answer:!0},{text:`Corals always die after bleaching.`,answer:!1},{text:`Bleaching is caused by water that is too cold.`,answer:!1},{text:`Scientists say reducing emissions is the most important solution.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-054`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'the picture is more complicated' and experiments showed 'little difference', 'what you eat matters more than when', eggs and porridge 'keep you full for longer', and experts 'still recommend a healthy breakfast' for children.`,passage:`Breakfast has long been called the most important meal of the day, but recent research suggests the picture is more complicated. Some studies found that people who eat breakfast tend to be slimmer, but this may be because they also have other healthy habits. In controlled experiments, where people were told either to eat breakfast or to skip it, there was little difference in weight after several months. Nutritionists now say that what you eat matters more than when you eat it. A breakfast of sugary cereal or pastries may leave you hungry again by mid-morning, while eggs, yoghurt or porridge can keep you full for longer. For children, however, most experts still recommend a healthy breakfast, as it seems to help concentration at school.`,statements:[{text:`Research clearly proves that breakfast helps people lose weight.`,answer:!1},{text:`Nutritionists think the type of food is more important than the timing.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sugary cereal keeps people full for longer than porridge.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most experts still recommend breakfast for children.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-055`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says napping in Japan is 'not usually seen as a sign of laziness', elsewhere a worker 'might be told off', some firms 'have installed special sleeping pods', and longer sleep 'may leave people feeling worse'.`,passage:`In Japan, it is common for people to take a short nap at their desks or on trains, and this is not usually seen as a sign of laziness. In fact, some people believe that sleeping at work shows that a person is so dedicated that they are exhausted. In other countries, attitudes are very different, and an employee caught sleeping at their desk might be told off by their manager. However, more and more companies around the world are recognising the benefits of short naps. Some technology firms have installed special sleeping pods where staff can rest for twenty minutes. Researchers say a nap of this length can improve alertness and performance, but sleeping for longer may leave people feeling worse.`,statements:[{text:`In Japan, napping at work is usually considered lazy.`,answer:!1},{text:`In some countries, a worker might be criticised for sleeping at their desk.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some companies have installed pods where staff can sleep.`,answer:!0},{text:`Researchers say long naps are better than short ones.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-056`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the market dates from 'the fourteenth century', it 'had shrunk to just a handful of stalls', 'a group of young residents' saved it, and it 'now has over sixty stalls'.`,passage:`The town of Greystone has had a weekly market in its main square since the fourteenth century. For hundreds of years, farmers came from the surrounding villages to sell vegetables, cheese and animals. By the early 2000s, however, the market had shrunk to just a handful of stalls, as most people preferred to shop in supermarkets. A group of young residents decided to try to save it. They invited local producers, artists and street food sellers, and organised live music every Saturday. The changes were a huge success, and the market now has over sixty stalls. Some older residents feel it has become too focused on tourists, but most agree that the square is livelier than it has been for decades.`,statements:[{text:`The market in Greystone is less than a hundred years old.`,answer:!1},{text:`By the early 2000s the market had very few stalls.`,answer:!0},{text:`Older residents organised the changes to the market.`,answer:!1},{text:`The market now has more than sixty stalls.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-057`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says antibiotics were 'first used in the 1940s', colds and flu are caused by viruses 'which antibiotics cannot treat', antibiotics are 'given to farm animals', and simple infections 'could once again become deadly'.`,passage:`Antibiotics have saved millions of lives since they were first used in the 1940s. However, doctors are increasingly worried about antibiotic resistance, which happens when bacteria change and the medicines stop working against them. One of the main causes is the overuse of antibiotics. Many people take them for colds and flu, even though these illnesses are caused by viruses, which antibiotics cannot treat. In some countries, antibiotics are also given to farm animals to make them grow faster. Health organisations are asking doctors to prescribe antibiotics only when they are really needed and are encouraging patients to finish every course they are prescribed. Without action, experts warn, simple infections could once again become deadly.`,statements:[{text:`Antibiotics have been used since the 1940s.`,answer:!0},{text:`Antibiotics are effective against colds and flu.`,answer:!1},{text:`In some countries, farm animals are given antibiotics.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts say simple infections could become dangerous again.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-058`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she 'had never been good at saving money', she 'was shocked' by her spending, the transfer happened 'on the day she was paid', and she 'had saved enough for a short holiday'.`,passage:`Nina had never been good at saving money. Every month, her salary seemed to disappear before she knew where it had gone. After she found herself unable to pay for a car repair, she decided something had to change. She started writing down everything she spent, and she was shocked to discover that she was spending almost two hundred euros a month on takeaway food and coffee. She began cooking at home and taking a flask of coffee to work. She also set up an automatic transfer so that a fixed amount went into a savings account on the day she was paid. A year later, she had saved enough for a short holiday and still had money left for emergencies.`,statements:[{text:`Nina had always been careful with her money.`,answer:!1},{text:`Nina was surprised by how much she spent on takeaway food and coffee.`,answer:!0},{text:`Money was moved into her savings account at the end of each month.`,answer:!1},{text:`After a year Nina could afford a holiday.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-059`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Naismith was 'a Canadian teacher', he wanted a game to play 'indoors', someone had to 'climb a ladder to get the ball out', and players 'had to pass it from where they stood'.`,passage:`Basketball was invented in 1891 by James Naismith, a Canadian teacher working in the United States. He wanted to create a game that his students could play indoors during the cold winter months. The first baskets were actually peach baskets, and every time a player scored, someone had to climb a ladder to get the ball out. It was several years before someone thought of cutting a hole in the bottom. The original rules did not allow players to run with the ball; they had to pass it from where they stood. Since then, the game has spread around the world and is now one of the most popular sports, especially in the United States, China and parts of Europe.`,statements:[{text:`Basketball was invented by an American teacher.`,answer:!1},{text:`Naismith wanted a game that could be played indoors.`,answer:!0},{text:`The first baskets had holes in the bottom.`,answer:!1},{text:`In the original rules, players could not run with the ball.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-060`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says trees 'can lower temperatures in streets', they reduce 'the risk of flooding', people 'wrote love letters and poems', and the aim is to go 'from around twenty per cent to forty per cent'.`,passage:`Many cities are planting more trees to help them cope with hotter summers. Trees provide shade and release water into the air, which can lower temperatures in streets by several degrees. They also absorb rainwater, reducing the risk of flooding, and improve air quality. In Melbourne, the city council has created an online map showing every tree in the city, with information about its age and type. Residents were invited to send emails to individual trees to report problems, but to the council's surprise, thousands of people wrote love letters and poems to their favourite trees instead. The council's aim is to increase the area of the city covered by trees from around twenty per cent to forty per cent.`,statements:[{text:`Trees can help to reduce temperatures in city streets.`,answer:!0},{text:`Trees increase the risk of flooding.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many people in Melbourne sent emails to trees that were not about problems.`,answer:!0},{text:`Melbourne plans to double its tree cover.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-061`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people can buy outfits 'more often than ever before', the industry 'uses huge amounts of water', some brands 'have also started repair services', and critics say the efforts 'are small'.`,passage:`Fast fashion, the production of cheap, trendy clothes in large quantities, has made it possible for people to buy new outfits more often than ever before. However, the environmental cost is enormous. The fashion industry uses huge amounts of water and is responsible for a significant share of global carbon emissions. Many cheap clothes are worn only a few times before being thrown away. In response, a growing number of consumers are turning to second-hand shops, clothing rental services and clothes-swapping events. Some brands have also started repair services or offer discounts to customers who return old clothes for recycling. Critics argue, though, that these efforts are small compared with the number of new garments still being produced every year.`,statements:[{text:`Fast fashion allows people to buy new clothes more frequently.`,answer:!0},{text:`The fashion industry uses very little water.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some brands offer repair services.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics believe brands have already solved the problem.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-062`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she 'found the advice difficult to follow', members 'shared recipes', she walks 'every morning', and 'the support of others made all the difference'.`,passage:`When Priya was diagnosed with diabetes at the age of thirty, she was frightened and confused. Her doctor explained that she would need to change her diet and take more exercise, but she found the advice difficult to follow. Things started to improve when she joined an online community of people with the same condition. Members shared recipes, gave each other tips on managing blood sugar and offered support on bad days. Inspired by them, Priya began walking for an hour every morning and learned to cook meals with less sugar. Two years later, her condition is well controlled, and she now runs a blog with healthy recipes. She says the support of others made all the difference.`,statements:[{text:`Priya found it easy to follow her doctor's advice at first.`,answer:!1},{text:`Members of the online community shared recipes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Priya now walks for an hour every evening.`,answer:!1},{text:`Priya believes support from other people was very important.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-063`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they are 'still far from common', 'most crashes are caused by human error', the technology 'struggles' in heavy snow, and 'it is not clear who should be responsible'.`,passage:`Self-driving cars have been tested on public roads for several years, but they are still far from common. Supporters say they could reduce accidents, since most crashes are caused by human error, such as tiredness or using a phone while driving. They could also give more independence to people who cannot drive, including elderly and disabled people. However, the technology still struggles in certain situations, such as heavy snow, roadworks or busy city centres with unpredictable pedestrians. There are also legal questions: if a self-driving car causes an accident, it is not clear who should be responsible, the owner or the manufacturer. Most experts believe that fully self-driving cars will not be widespread for at least another decade.`,statements:[{text:`Self-driving cars are already common on most roads.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most car accidents are caused by human mistakes.`,answer:!0},{text:`The technology works perfectly in heavy snow.`,answer:!1},{text:`It is unclear who is responsible when a self-driving car causes an accident.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-064`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says there are 'cheap tickets for young people', some performances are 'shown live in cinemas', operas have been staged in 'swimming pools', and most companies 'still depend on government money'.`,passage:`Opera is often seen as an expensive form of entertainment for older and wealthy audiences. To change this image, several opera houses have started offering cheap tickets for young people. In London, people under twenty-five can buy some seats for as little as ten pounds, and certain performances are shown live in cinemas around the world. Some companies have also staged operas in unusual places, such as car parks, swimming pools and old factories, to attract people who would never enter a traditional theatre. These experiments have had some success, with audiences becoming younger and more varied. However, opera remains expensive to produce, since it requires large orchestras, singers and elaborate costumes, so most companies still depend on government money and donations.`,statements:[{text:`Some opera houses offer cheap tickets for young people.`,answer:!0},{text:`Opera performances are never shown in cinemas.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some operas have been performed in swimming pools.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most opera companies no longer need government money.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-065`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says roads 'became impassable' in the rainy season, orders are placed 'by text message', the project 'began in Rwanda', and drones fly 'except during very strong storms'.`,passage:`In some rural parts of Africa, getting medical supplies to remote clinics used to take hours or even days, especially during the rainy season when roads became impassable. Now, drones are being used to deliver blood, vaccines and medicines within minutes. Health workers place an order by text message, and the supplies are dropped by parachute near the clinic. The project began in Rwanda in 2016 and has since expanded to several other countries. Doctors say the faster deliveries have helped save the lives of many mothers who lost a lot of blood after giving birth. The drones are launched from a central base and can fly more than a hundred kilometres in any weather, except during very strong storms.`,statements:[{text:`Roads to some clinics can become impossible to use in the rainy season.`,answer:!0},{text:`Health workers order supplies by email.`,answer:!1},{text:`The drone project started in Rwanda.`,answer:!0},{text:`The drones can fly in very strong storms.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-066`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says tourists are 'far higher than the number of residents', the population 'has fallen sharply', there is 'an entry fee for day visitors on the busiest days', and some residents think the measures 'do not go far enough'.`,passage:`For centuries, Venice has attracted visitors with its canals, bridges and beautiful buildings. In recent years, however, the city has struggled with overtourism. On busy days, the number of tourists is far higher than the number of residents, and narrow streets become so crowded that it is difficult to walk. The local population has fallen sharply, as many residents have moved to the mainland because of high rents and the loss of ordinary shops. To manage the situation, the city introduced an entry fee for day visitors on the busiest days and banned large cruise ships from entering the historic centre. Some residents believe the measures do not go far enough, while some business owners fear they will lose income.`,statements:[{text:`On busy days there are more tourists than residents in Venice.`,answer:!0},{text:`The number of people living in Venice has increased.`,answer:!1},{text:`Day visitors must pay a fee on some days.`,answer:!0},{text:`All residents think the new measures are too strict.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-067`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says some researchers argue 'games are not always harmful', games can improve 'the ability to work in a team', parents should 'occasionally play with them', and consoles should be kept 'out of bedrooms'.`,passage:`Many parents worry that their children spend too much time playing video games. However, some researchers argue that games are not always harmful. Certain games can improve problem-solving skills, hand-eye coordination and the ability to work in a team. Some schools even use games to teach subjects like history and maths. The key, according to experts, is balance. Children who play for several hours every day may sleep less, do less exercise and spend less time with friends in person. Experts suggest that parents should know which games their children are playing, set clear limits and occasionally play with them. They also advise keeping games consoles out of bedrooms, so that playing time is easier to control.`,statements:[{text:`Researchers agree that all video games are harmful.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some games can help children work in a team.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts suggest parents should sometimes play games with their children.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts recommend keeping consoles in children's bedrooms.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-068`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he was 'more interested in cooking the fish than catching it', he trained 'in Milan', 'the menu changes daily', and he 'refuses to serve species that are in danger'.`,passage:`Marco grew up in a family of fishermen on the coast of Sicily, but as a teenager he was more interested in cooking the fish than catching it. After finishing school, he moved to Milan to train as a chef, working in several famous restaurants. Ten years later, he returned to his hometown and opened a small restaurant by the harbour. Every morning he buys fish directly from the boats, including from his own father, and the menu changes daily depending on what has been caught. He refuses to serve species that are in danger from overfishing, even when customers ask for them. Last year the restaurant was included in a well-known food guide, and it is now usually necessary to book weeks in advance.`,statements:[{text:`As a teenager, Marco wanted to become a fisherman.`,answer:!1},{text:`Marco trained as a chef in Milan.`,answer:!0},{text:`The menu at Marco's restaurant is the same every day.`,answer:!1},{text:`Marco will not serve fish that are threatened by overfishing.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-069`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says conversations 'are more relaxed', walking 'can increase creative thinking', they are 'not suitable for every situation', and they work best 'for small groups of two or three people'.`,passage:`Walking meetings, in which colleagues discuss work while taking a walk instead of sitting in an office, are becoming more popular. People who have tried them say they feel more creative and that conversations are more relaxed, partly because they are walking side by side rather than facing each other across a table. Studies have shown that walking can increase creative thinking, at least for a short time afterwards. Walking meetings also help people who spend most of the day sitting down to move more. However, they are not suitable for every situation. They work best for small groups of two or three people and are not ideal when notes need to be taken or documents need to be looked at.`,statements:[{text:`People say walking meetings feel more relaxed.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies show that walking may increase creative thinking.`,answer:!0},{text:`Walking meetings are suitable for every kind of meeting.`,answer:!1},{text:`Walking meetings work best with large groups.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-070`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the trees 'absorb huge amounts of carbon dioxide', many species 'have not yet been studied', forest has been cut 'to make room for cattle farms', and deforestation is 'much lower' in indigenous areas.`,passage:`The Amazon rainforest is often called the lungs of the planet because its trees absorb huge amounts of carbon dioxide. It is also home to around ten per cent of all known species on Earth, many of which have not yet been studied by scientists. However, large areas of the forest have been cut down to make room for cattle farms and crops such as soya. Some scientists warn that if deforestation continues, parts of the Amazon could turn into dry grassland, releasing carbon instead of storing it. Indigenous communities, who have lived in the forest for thousands of years, play an important role in protecting it, and studies show that deforestation rates are much lower in areas they control.`,statements:[{text:`The Amazon's trees absorb a lot of carbon dioxide.`,answer:!0},{text:`All species in the Amazon have already been studied.`,answer:!1},{text:`Forest has been cleared to make space for cattle farms.`,answer:!0},{text:`Deforestation is higher in areas controlled by indigenous communities.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-071`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the school 'hardly gives any' homework, pupils 'perform very well in national tests', there is 'a fifteen-minute break after every lesson', and the methods 'might not work everywhere'.`,passage:`A school in Finland has become famous for its unusual approach to homework: it hardly gives any. Teachers believe that children learn best during the school day and that evenings should be for family, sport and play. Despite this, the school's pupils perform very well in national tests. The head teacher explains that lessons are carefully planned and that classes are small, so teachers can give individual attention to each pupil. Pupils also have a fifteen-minute break after every lesson, which teachers say helps them concentrate. Visitors from many countries have come to observe the school, but the head teacher warns that its methods might not work everywhere, as they depend on well-trained teachers and strong public trust in schools.`,statements:[{text:`The school gives pupils a lot of homework.`,answer:!1},{text:`Pupils at the school do well in national tests.`,answer:!0},{text:`Pupils have a short break after every lesson.`,answer:!0},{text:`The head teacher believes the methods would work in any country.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-072`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they spent 'only about fifteen minutes at the top', the climb 'would have been impossible without Tenzing', they 'refused to answer', and Hillary built 'schools and hospitals' in Nepal.`,passage:`In 1953, Edmund Hillary from New Zealand and Tenzing Norgay, a Sherpa from Nepal, became the first people confirmed to have reached the summit of Mount Everest. They spent only about fifteen minutes at the top, taking photographs and leaving small gifts in the snow. Hillary later said that the climb would have been impossible without Tenzing's experience and strength. For years, journalists kept asking which of them had stepped onto the summit first, but the two men refused to answer, saying they had reached it together as a team. After the expedition, Hillary spent much of his life helping the Sherpa people, building schools and hospitals in the mountain villages of Nepal.`,statements:[{text:`Hillary and Tenzing stayed at the summit for several hours.`,answer:!1},{text:`Hillary believed Tenzing's help was essential to the climb.`,answer:!0},{text:`The two men told journalists which of them reached the summit first.`,answer:!1},{text:`Hillary later built schools in Nepal.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-073`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says sales 'have risen steadily', there are 'cafés where customers can pay to play', some games 'take several hours to complete', and fans 'pay in advance'.`,passage:`Board games, which many people thought would disappear in the age of video games, have enjoyed a remarkable comeback. Sales have risen steadily over the past decade, and cafés where customers can pay to play from a large collection of games have opened in cities around the world. Many players say they enjoy the chance to spend time with friends face to face, away from screens. Modern board games are also very different from the classic family games of the past. Many involve strategy, cooperation and complex stories, and some take several hours to complete. Designers often raise money for new games through crowdfunding websites, where fans pay in advance to help produce games they would like to see made.`,statements:[{text:`Board game sales have fallen in the last ten years.`,answer:!1},{text:`There are cafés where people pay to play board games.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some modern board games take several hours to finish.`,answer:!0},{text:`Fans sometimes pay in advance to help produce new games.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-074`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'water rose so quickly' that people were rescued by boat, volunteers 'came from nearby towns', the restaurant 'cooked free meals', and last winter 'no homes were flooded'.`,passage:`When a severe flood hit the town of Ashford Bridge two winters ago, dozens of homes and shops were badly damaged. Water rose so quickly that many residents had to be rescued by boat. In the weeks that followed, hundreds of volunteers came from nearby towns to help clean houses and remove ruined furniture. A local restaurant cooked free meals every day for families who had lost their kitchens. The disaster also led to changes: the council built new flood barriers along the river and planted trees on the hills above the town to slow down rainwater. Last winter, when heavy rain fell again, the barriers held, and no homes were flooded. Residents say the experience brought the community closer together.`,statements:[{text:`The water rose slowly, giving residents plenty of time to leave.`,answer:!1},{text:`Volunteers from other towns helped after the flood.`,answer:!0},{text:`A restaurant charged families for meals after the flood.`,answer:!1},{text:`No homes were flooded during last winter's heavy rain.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-075`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says spices 'were traded like gold', 'only the wealthy could afford them', spices were 'one of the main reasons for the great voyages', and saffron is 'one of the most expensive foods'.`,passage:`Spices were once so valuable that they were traded like gold. In the Middle Ages, pepper, cinnamon and nutmeg were brought to Europe from Asia along long and dangerous routes, and their price increased greatly at every stage of the journey. Only the wealthy could afford them, and serving dishes full of spices was a way of showing off one's wealth. The desire to find a direct sea route to the countries where spices grew was one of the main reasons for the great voyages of exploration in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. Today, spices are cheap and available in every supermarket, but saffron, which is collected by hand from flowers, is still one of the most expensive foods in the world.`,statements:[{text:`Spices were once very valuable.`,answer:!0},{text:`In the Middle Ages, spices were cheap for ordinary people.`,answer:!1},{text:`The search for spices encouraged voyages of exploration.`,answer:!0},{text:`Saffron is now one of the cheapest spices.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-076`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'fewer people borrow printed books', some libraries 'lend unusual items such as tools, musical instruments', librarians 'spend much of their time helping people with technology', and some 'reduce opening hours'.`,passage:`Public libraries have had to reinvent themselves in the digital age. Fewer people borrow printed books than in the past, but many libraries are busier than ever because they now offer much more. Visitors can use computers and free Wi-Fi, attend language classes, borrow e-books and even use 3D printers. Some libraries lend unusual items such as tools, musical instruments and board games. For many people who cannot afford internet access at home, the library is the only place where they can apply for jobs online or contact government services. Librarians, therefore, spend much of their time helping people with technology. Despite their importance, many libraries have faced budget cuts, and some have been forced to reduce opening hours or close altogether.`,statements:[{text:`Fewer people borrow printed books than before.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some libraries lend tools and musical instruments.`,answer:!0},{text:`Librarians rarely help people with technology.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some libraries have reduced their opening hours.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-077`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the job involves 'far more waiting than taking pictures', she waited 'eleven days', she is paid 'only when magazines or companies buy her pictures', and she advises studying 'animal behaviour'.`,passage:`Hannah works as a wildlife photographer, a job many people consider a dream. However, she explains that it involves far more waiting than taking pictures. To photograph a rare bird in Scotland last year, she spent eleven days hiding in a small shelter, often in freezing rain, before the bird finally appeared. She also has to spend a lot of time on less exciting tasks, such as editing photos, writing proposals and managing her finances. Income is uncertain, since she is paid only when magazines or companies buy her pictures. Despite the difficulties, Hannah says she cannot imagine doing anything else. Her advice to young people who want to follow the same path is to study animal behaviour, not just photography.`,statements:[{text:`Hannah spends most of her time actually taking photographs.`,answer:!1},{text:`She waited for less than a week to photograph the rare bird.`,answer:!1},{text:`Hannah has a fixed monthly salary.`,answer:!1},{text:`She advises young photographers to learn about animal behaviour.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-078`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says designers want to keep attention 'for as long as possible', guests 'hand in their devices', participants 'feel anxious for the first day or two', and 'many find it hard to keep their new habits'.`,passage:`Most people check their phones dozens of times a day, often without even realising it. App designers use techniques such as notifications, likes and endless scrolling to keep users' attention for as long as possible. In response, a growing number of people are trying a 'digital detox', spending a weekend or even a whole week without their phones. Some hotels and holiday companies now offer detox breaks where guests hand in their devices when they arrive. Participants often say they feel anxious for the first day or two, but then begin to enjoy reading, walking and talking to other people. However, many find it hard to keep their new habits once they return to their normal routine.`,statements:[{text:`App designers try to keep users' attention for a long time.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some hotels ask guests to hand in their phones.`,answer:!0},{text:`Participants usually feel relaxed from the very first day.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most people easily keep their new habits after a detox.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-079`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says historians 'are not sure', the modern Games were 'first held in Athens in 1896', the distance was 'not fixed until 1908', and it was chosen to 'finish in front of the royal family's box'.`,passage:`The marathon gets its name from a Greek legend. According to the story, in 490 BC a messenger ran about forty kilometres from the town of Marathon to Athens to announce that the Greeks had won a battle against the Persians, and then died from exhaustion. Historians are not sure whether the story is true. When the modern Olympic Games were first held in Athens in 1896, organisers created a race following the same route to honour the legend. The distance was not fixed until 1908, when the London Olympics set it at 42.195 kilometres, reportedly so that the race could finish in front of the royal family's box in the stadium.`,statements:[{text:`Historians are certain that the legend of the messenger is true.`,answer:!1},{text:`The modern Olympic Games were first held in Athens.`,answer:!0},{text:`The official marathon distance was fixed in 1896.`,answer:!1},{text:`The 1908 distance was reportedly chosen so the race could finish near the royal box.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-080`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says allergies seem 'more common, especially in wealthy countries', farm children 'are less likely to develop allergies', doctors 'used to advise' avoiding peanuts, and early introduction 'reduces the risk'.`,passage:`Allergies seem to be becoming more common, especially in wealthy countries. One theory, known as the hygiene hypothesis, suggests that children who grow up in very clean environments do not come into contact with enough germs, so their immune systems overreact to harmless things like pollen or peanuts. Studies have found that children who grow up on farms, surrounded by animals, are less likely to develop allergies. For peanut allergy, doctors used to advise parents to avoid giving peanuts to babies. However, recent research showed that introducing small amounts of peanut early in life actually reduces the risk. Parents are now advised to speak to a doctor before doing this, particularly if their child already has other allergies.`,statements:[{text:`Allergies appear to be increasing in wealthy countries.`,answer:!0},{text:`Children who grow up on farms are more likely to have allergies.`,answer:!1},{text:`Doctors have always advised giving peanuts to babies.`,answer:!1},{text:`Introducing peanuts early may reduce the risk of allergy.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-081`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says many people told him 'bookshops had no future', he did not try 'to compete with online stores on price', staff 'write personal recommendations', and he is only 'thinking of opening a second one'.`,passage:`Bruno opened a bookshop in a quiet street in Lisbon at a time when many people told him that bookshops had no future. Instead of trying to compete with online stores on price, he focused on creating a place where people wanted to spend time. The shop has comfortable chairs, a small café and a garden at the back. Bruno and his staff read widely and write personal recommendations on cards placed next to the books. Every week, the shop hosts readings, book clubs and children's storytelling sessions. Customers often say that they discover books there that they would never have found online. Eight years after opening, the shop is profitable, and Bruno is thinking of opening a second one in Porto.`,statements:[{text:`Everyone told Bruno that his bookshop would be a great success.`,answer:!1},{text:`Bruno tried to offer lower prices than online stores.`,answer:!1},{text:`Staff write personal recommendations for books.`,answer:!0},{text:`Bruno has already opened a second shop in Porto.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-082`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says plants 'grow without soil', farms use 'up to ninety-five per cent less water', they 'use large amounts of electricity', and growing wheat this way 'is not yet economical'.`,passage:`Vertical farming is a method of growing crops indoors in stacked layers, often inside old warehouses or purpose-built towers. Plants grow without soil, with their roots in water containing nutrients, and LED lights replace the sun. Supporters say vertical farms use up to ninety-five per cent less water than traditional farms and need no pesticides. Because they can be built in cities, food does not need to travel long distances to reach customers. However, the farms use large amounts of electricity for lighting and temperature control, which makes them expensive to run. At present, they mostly grow salad leaves and herbs, which grow quickly and sell at high prices. Growing staple crops such as wheat or rice this way is not yet economical.`,statements:[{text:`In vertical farms, plants grow in soil.`,answer:!1},{text:`Vertical farms use much less water than traditional farms.`,answer:!0},{text:`Vertical farms are cheap to run because they need little electricity.`,answer:!1},{text:`Growing wheat in vertical farms is currently not economical.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-083`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says this comes 'from a desire to be liked', saying yes 'can lead to stress', experts suggest saying 'you need to check your schedule' instead of answering immediately, and people 'respect colleagues who set clear limits'.`,passage:`Many people find it difficult to say no when colleagues ask for help, even when they are already very busy. According to psychologists, this often comes from a desire to be liked or a fear of seeming lazy. However, always saying yes can lead to stress, long hours and poor-quality work. Experts suggest a few simple strategies. Instead of answering immediately, you can say you need to check your schedule, which gives you time to think. If you do decide to refuse, it helps to be polite but clear and, if possible, to suggest an alternative, such as helping later in the week. Psychologists also point out that people generally respect colleagues who set clear limits.`,statements:[{text:`Some people find it hard to refuse requests because they want to be liked.`,answer:!0},{text:`Always agreeing to help can lead to stress.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts recommend always answering requests immediately.`,answer:!1},{text:`People generally respect colleagues who set clear limits.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-084`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says companies 'have taken paying customers', passengers experience 'a few minutes of weightlessness', 'only the very rich can afford them', and supporters compare it to 'air travel in the early twentieth century'.`,passage:`Space tourism, once only a dream, has started to become a reality. In recent years, several private companies have taken paying customers on short flights to the edge of space, where they experience a few minutes of weightlessness and see the curve of the Earth. Tickets cost hundreds of thousands of dollars, so for now only the very rich can afford them. Supporters argue that, just like air travel in the early twentieth century, prices will eventually fall. Critics, however, point out that rocket launches produce significant pollution and question whether it is right to spend so much money on such trips when there are serious problems on Earth. Some scientists also worry about the growing amount of rubbish orbiting the planet.`,statements:[{text:`Private companies have taken paying customers to the edge of space.`,answer:!0},{text:`Passengers experience several hours of weightlessness.`,answer:!1},{text:`Space flights are currently affordable for most people.`,answer:!1},{text:`Supporters compare space tourism to early air travel.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-085`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says her family 'was shocked', she started baking 'to relax', she took a course 'in Paris', and her mother 'helps out at the counter on busy weekends'.`,passage:`Kim was a successful lawyer in Seoul when she decided to give up her career to become a professional baker. Her family was shocked, as they had spent a lot of money on her education. Kim had started baking to relax after long days at work, and she soon found that she enjoyed it far more than her job. She took a six-month course in Paris, then returned home and opened a small bakery specialising in French pastries with Korean flavours, such as green tea and sweet red beans. At first, her parents refused to visit the shop. Now, however, they are among her most loyal customers, and her mother helps out at the counter on busy weekends.`,statements:[{text:`Kim's family was surprised by her decision.`,answer:!0},{text:`Kim started baking as a way to relax.`,answer:!0},{text:`Kim studied baking in Seoul.`,answer:!1},{text:`Kim's mother sometimes works in the bakery.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-086`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says wolves 'disappeared from many parts of Europe', they appear 'a few kilometres from large cities', experts 'welcome their return', and governments help farmers 'buy electric fences'.`,passage:`Wolves were hunted until they disappeared from many parts of Europe during the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. In recent decades, however, thanks to legal protection, they have returned to countries such as Germany, France and Italy, sometimes appearing just a few kilometres from large cities. Wildlife experts welcome their return, saying that wolves help keep populations of deer and wild boar under control, which allows forests to recover. Farmers, on the other hand, are worried, because wolves sometimes kill sheep and goats. Governments now pay compensation for animals killed by wolves and help farmers buy electric fences and guard dogs. The debate remains heated, and in some areas there have been calls to allow limited hunting again.`,statements:[{text:`Wolves had disappeared from many parts of Europe in the past.`,answer:!0},{text:`Wolves never come close to large cities.`,answer:!1},{text:`Wildlife experts are worried about the return of wolves.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some governments help farmers buy electric fences.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-087`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people 'are living longer', 'over forty per cent of grandparents provide regular childcare', daily childcare 'can be exhausting', and some grandparents 'feel they cannot refuse'.`,passage:`In many countries, people are living longer than ever before. As a result, more and more grandparents are helping to look after their grandchildren while parents work. Surveys suggest that in some European countries, over forty per cent of grandparents provide regular childcare. Many say they enjoy spending time with the children and feel it keeps them active. Some studies have even found that grandparents who help occasionally tend to live longer. However, looking after young children every day can be exhausting, especially for older people with health problems. Some grandparents also feel they cannot refuse, even when they would prefer to travel or pursue their own hobbies during retirement.`,statements:[{text:`People in many countries are living longer.`,answer:!0},{text:`In some European countries, more than forty per cent of grandparents provide regular childcare.`,answer:!0},{text:`Looking after children every day is never tiring for grandparents.`,answer:!1},{text:`All grandparents are happy to provide childcare whenever they are asked.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-088`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the Maya and Aztecs 'drank cacao', beans 'were used as money', bars 'were not produced until the nineteenth century', and most cacao 'is grown in West Africa'.`,passage:`Chocolate comes from the seeds of the cacao tree, which grows in hot, humid regions near the equator. The ancient Maya and Aztecs drank cacao as a bitter, spicy drink, and cacao beans were so valuable that they were used as money. When chocolate arrived in Europe in the sixteenth century, sugar was added to make it sweeter, and it became a fashionable drink among the rich. Solid chocolate bars were not produced until the nineteenth century. Today, most of the world's cacao is grown in West Africa, mainly by small farmers, many of whom earn very little. Some chocolate companies now pay farmers higher prices through fair trade schemes, though critics say much more needs to be done.`,statements:[{text:`The ancient Maya and Aztecs ate chocolate in bars.`,answer:!1},{text:`Cacao beans were once used as money.`,answer:!0},{text:`Solid chocolate bars were first made in the sixteenth century.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most cacao today is grown in West Africa.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-089`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Frank expected 'about twenty people' but 'more than a hundred and fifty turned up', the council agreed 'to close the road', residents 'set up a group chat', and the garden was started 'since then'.`,passage:`Last spring, the residents of Elm Street, a busy road in a large city, decided they were tired of not knowing their neighbours. One of them, a retired teacher called Frank, printed leaflets inviting everyone to a street party. He expected about twenty people to come, but more than a hundred and fifty turned up, bringing food from many different countries. The council agreed to close the road to traffic for the afternoon. Since then, the residents have set up a group chat where they share tools, offer to water each other's plants and warn each other about problems. They have also started a small community garden on an empty piece of land at the end of the street.`,statements:[{text:`Frank expected far more people than actually came.`,answer:!1},{text:`The road was closed to traffic for the party.`,answer:!0},{text:`Residents now use a group chat to help each other.`,answer:!0},{text:`The community garden was created before the street party.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-090`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says dogs 'are descended from wolves', following a pointing finger is 'something even chimpanzees find difficult', both 'produce a hormone linked to love and trust', and dogs detect 'illnesses by smell'.`,passage:`Dogs have lived alongside humans for at least fifteen thousand years, and scientists believe they are descended from wolves that began to stay close to human camps, perhaps to eat leftover food. Over time, humans bred dogs for different purposes, such as hunting, herding sheep and guarding homes. Research has shown that dogs are particularly good at understanding human signals; for example, they can follow a pointing finger, something even chimpanzees find difficult. Studies have also found that when dogs and their owners look into each other's eyes, both produce a hormone linked to love and trust. Today, dogs work in many roles, from guiding blind people to detecting illnesses by smell.`,statements:[{text:`Scientists believe dogs are descended from wolves.`,answer:!0},{text:`Chimpanzees are better than dogs at following a pointing finger.`,answer:!1},{text:`Looking into each other's eyes produces a hormone in both dogs and owners.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some dogs can detect illnesses by smell.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-091`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says video is used 'for the first stage', recorded interviews happen 'without anyone watching live', light should be 'in front of them, not behind', and candidates should dress 'just as you would for a face-to-face interview'.`,passage:`Job interviews are increasingly being carried out by video, especially for the first stage of recruitment. Some companies even use recorded interviews, in which candidates answer questions in front of a camera without anyone watching live. Recruitment experts say that preparing for a video interview requires some extra thought. Candidates should test their camera and microphone in advance, choose a quiet room with a plain background and make sure the light is in front of them, not behind. It is also important to look at the camera rather than at the screen, so that the interviewer feels you are making eye contact. Experts recommend dressing just as you would for a face-to-face interview, even if you are at home.`,statements:[{text:`Video interviews are mainly used for the final stage of recruitment.`,answer:!1},{text:`In recorded interviews, someone is always watching live.`,answer:!1},{text:`Candidates should sit with the light behind them.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts advise dressing as you would for a face-to-face interview.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-092`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Singapore has 'very little land', 'around eighty per cent of residents live in public housing', owning a car is 'extremely expensive', and new buildings must 'include plants and gardens'.`,passage:`Singapore is often held up as an example of a city that has managed rapid growth successfully. Despite having very little land, it has some of the best housing, public transport and green spaces in Asia. Around eighty per cent of residents live in public housing flats, which are built by the government and sold to citizens at affordable prices. To reduce traffic, the city charges drivers for using busy roads at peak times, and owning a car is extremely expensive. The city has also introduced rules requiring new buildings to include plants and gardens, sometimes on their roofs and walls. Critics, however, point out that the government controls many aspects of daily life very strictly.`,statements:[{text:`Singapore has a lot of spare land for building.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most residents live in public housing.`,answer:!0},{text:`Owning a car in Singapore is cheap.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some new buildings must include gardens.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-093`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says she stopped 'after a shoulder injury', she 'hardly swam at all' for almost twenty years, she swims 'all year round', and her Swiss swim was 'to raise money for a children's hospital'.`,passage:`Rachel had been a talented swimmer as a child, but she stopped competing at sixteen after a shoulder injury. For almost twenty years she hardly swam at all. Then, at the age of thirty-five, a friend persuaded her to try open-water swimming in a nearby lake. She was nervous at first, as the water was cold and she could not see the bottom, but she soon fell in love with the feeling of freedom. She now swims in the lake all year round, even in winter, when the water temperature can drop to four degrees. Last summer she swam across a ten-kilometre lake in Switzerland to raise money for a children's hospital, and she is now planning to swim the English Channel.`,statements:[{text:`Rachel stopped competing because of an injury.`,answer:!0},{text:`Rachel swam regularly between the ages of sixteen and thirty-five.`,answer:!1},{text:`Rachel only swims in the lake during the summer.`,answer:!1},{text:`Rachel's swim in Switzerland raised money for charity.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-094`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says this is 'partly determined by our genes', larks 'feel most alert in the morning', teenagers 'tend to become owls', and students' 'grades improved' at schools that started later.`,passage:`Most of us have a favourite time of day for working, and scientists say this is partly determined by our genes. Some people, often called 'larks', naturally wake up early and feel most alert in the morning. Others, known as 'owls', prefer to go to bed late and work best in the evening. Teenagers tend to become owls for a few years, which is one reason why many find early school starts so hard. Some schools that moved their start time later found that students were less tired and their grades improved. Because the traditional working day suits larks better, owls may feel tired and less productive, and some experts argue that employers should allow more flexible hours.`,statements:[{text:`Our genes partly influence our favourite time of day for working.`,answer:!0},{text:`Larks feel most alert in the evening.`,answer:!1},{text:`Teenagers often become owls for a few years.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some schools with later start times saw grades improve.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-095`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Medellín was 'one of the most dangerous cities', cable cars connect hillside areas 'with the city's metro system', 'crime has fallen significantly', and 'problems remain, such as inequality'.`,passage:`The city of Medellín, in Colombia, was once known as one of the most dangerous cities in the world. Since the 1990s, however, it has changed dramatically. One of the most important projects was the construction of cable cars connecting poor neighbourhoods on the steep hillsides with the city's metro system. Before this, residents had to walk for hours or take several buses to reach the city centre. The city also built libraries, parks and schools in these areas, often with impressive modern architecture. Crime has fallen significantly, and Medellín has won international awards for innovation. Problems remain, such as inequality and rising house prices, but the city is now a popular destination for tourists.`,statements:[{text:`Medellín was once considered very dangerous.`,answer:!0},{text:`The cable cars connect hillside neighbourhoods with the metro.`,answer:!0},{text:`Crime in Medellín has increased since the 1990s.`,answer:!1},{text:`Medellín no longer has any problems with inequality.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`b2-tf-096`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says programmes include 'protecting turtles on beaches', experts have 'raised concerns', organisations advise 'longer placements', and 'donating money can sometimes be more helpful'.`,passage:`Volunteering abroad has become popular among young people who want to travel and do something useful at the same time. Programmes range from teaching English to building schools or protecting turtles on beaches. However, some experts have raised concerns about so-called 'voluntourism'. They point out that short-term volunteers without proper skills may do work that local people could be paid to do, and that children in orphanages can be harmed when volunteers come and go every few weeks. Organisations that support responsible volunteering advise people to choose longer placements, check that the project is run with local communities and ask whether their skills are actually needed. They also suggest that donating money can sometimes be more helpful than volunteering in person.`,statements:[{text:`Volunteering programmes abroad include protecting turtles.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts say short-term volunteers always help local communities.`,answer:!1},{text:`Responsible organisations recommend choosing shorter placements.`,answer:!1},{text:`Donating money can sometimes be more useful than volunteering.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-097`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says some modern roads 'still follow the same routes', aqueducts used 'only gravity', the concrete 'became stronger over time', and researchers hope to make modern concrete 'more long-lasting'.`,passage:`The ancient Romans were remarkable engineers, and many of their structures are still standing today. Their roads were built in layers of stone and gravel, which made them strong enough to last for centuries, and some modern roads in Europe still follow the same routes. The Romans also built aqueducts to carry water over long distances to their cities, using only gravity. One of their greatest inventions was a type of concrete that has proved extremely durable. Scientists have discovered that Roman concrete used in harbours actually became stronger over time, as seawater reacted with volcanic ash in the mixture. Researchers are now studying it in the hope of making modern concrete more long-lasting and environmentally friendly.`,statements:[{text:`Some modern European roads follow Roman routes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Roman aqueducts used pumps to move water.`,answer:!1},{text:`Roman concrete in harbours became weaker over time.`,answer:!1},{text:`Scientists are studying Roman concrete to improve modern concrete.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-098`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says he had 'months of unsuccessful applications' and retrained, former colleagues 'thought he was making a mistake', the first weeks were 'exhausting', and he is 'particularly good at helping pupils who struggled with maths'.`,passage:`When Leo was made redundant from his job in a bank at the age of fifty-two, he worried that nobody would employ him again. After months of unsuccessful applications, he decided to retrain as a primary school teaching assistant, something he had always been curious about. Many of his former colleagues thought he was making a mistake, since the salary was much lower. Leo admits that the first few weeks were exhausting, as he was not used to the noise and energy of thirty children. However, he soon discovered that his patience and experience with numbers made him particularly good at helping pupils who struggled with maths. He says he has never enjoyed a job so much, even though he earns less.`,statements:[{text:`Leo found a new job in a bank soon after being made redundant.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some former colleagues thought his decision was a mistake.`,answer:!0},{text:`Leo found the first few weeks at school very relaxing.`,answer:!1},{text:`Leo is good at helping pupils who find maths difficult.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-099`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the Arctic tern 'holds the record for the longest migration', scientists are 'still not completely sure', birds seem to use 'the Earth's magnetic field', and many die from 'collisions with buildings, especially brightly lit ones'.`,passage:`Every year, millions of birds migrate thousands of kilometres between their summer and winter homes. The Arctic tern holds the record for the longest migration of any animal, flying from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back again each year, a round trip of around seventy thousand kilometres. Scientists are still not completely sure how birds find their way. They seem to use a combination of the position of the sun and stars, the Earth's magnetic field and familiar landmarks such as rivers and coastlines. Some birds can fly for days without stopping. Migration is extremely dangerous, however, and many birds die from exhaustion, storms or collisions with buildings, especially brightly lit ones at night.`,statements:[{text:`The Arctic tern makes the longest migration of any animal.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists fully understand how birds find their way.`,answer:!1},{text:`Birds may use the Earth's magnetic field to navigate.`,answer:!0},{text:`Brightly lit buildings are a danger to migrating birds.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`b2-tf-100`,type:`true-false`,level:`B2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says hockey is 'the national winter sport of Canada', parents drive children to 'early-morning practice', the cost 'has risen sharply', and programmes 'provide free second-hand equipment'.`,passage:`Ice hockey is the national winter sport of Canada, and for many Canadian children, learning to skate is almost as natural as learning to walk. In small towns across the country, the local ice rink is often the centre of community life during the long winters. Parents get up before dawn to drive their children to early-morning practice, and whole towns come out to watch local teams play at weekends. However, the cost of playing hockey has risen sharply. Equipment, ice time and travel to tournaments can cost families thousands of dollars a year, and some worry that the sport is becoming available only to wealthier families. Several programmes now provide free second-hand equipment to children whose families cannot afford it.`,statements:[{text:`Ice hockey is Canada's national winter sport.`,answer:!0},{text:`Hockey practice for children usually takes place late in the evening.`,answer:!1},{text:`The cost of playing hockey has fallen in recent years.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some programmes give free equipment to children from poorer families.`,answer:!0}]}]};export{e as default};